{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1533311722,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzNDc0NTAxMTk=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2732","origin":"github"},"title":"--proc-post doesn't play nicely with `create`?","message":"#### What is the problem?\n```shell\n$\u003e datalad --proc-post setup_yoda_dataset --cmd create yodads \n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/yodads \ncreate(ok): /tmp/yodads (dataset)                                                \n[ERROR  ] No dataset found [dataset.py:require_dataset:500] (NoDatasetArgumentFound) \nusage: datalad create [-h] [-f] [-D DESCRIPTION] [-d PATH] [--no-annex]\n                      [--nosave] [--annex-version ANNEX_VERSION]\n                      [--annex-backend ANNEX_BACKEND]\n                      [--native-metadata-type LABEL] [--shared-access MODE]\n                      [--git-opts STRING] [--annex-opts STRING]\n                      [--annex-init-opts STRING] [--text-no-annex]\n                      [--fake-dates]\n                      [PATH]\n```\ndatalad 0.10.2 \n\ncould be just a \"user error\", but my guess is that procedures assume to be ran in \"current dataset\" which I have none here (running under /tmp).  With `create`, I could argue, that it makes sense to run procedures in created dataset.  But I could also see some procedures to be intended to be run in 'parent' dataset.  So how could `create` be used with procedures with intent for them to be ran within the newly created dataset (which I guess would be more common usecase).","files":null}]}